<!-- Exercise: Detecting Increasing Order (uses vector indexing) -->
<exercise xml:id="if-activity-2">
	<title>Example 2: Detecting Increasing Order</title>

	<p>
		Suppose <pf>list</pf> is a vector of three numbers.
		Set <pf>in_order</pf> to <pf>true</pf> if the entries are in increasing order; otherwise set it to <pf>false</pf>.
	</p>

	<program language="matlab">
a = list(1);
b = list(2);
c = list(3);

if a &lt;= b &amp;&amp; b &lt;= c
		in_order = true;
else
		in_order = false;
end
	</program>

	<p>
		This pattern is common: the <pf>if</pf> block assigns <pf>true</pf>, and the <pf>else</pf> block assigns <pf>false</pf>.
		When you see this often enough, you start recognizing conditions as "tests" whose job is to set flags.
	</p>
</exercise>

<!-- Exercise: Minimum of Three Values (uses vector indexing) -->
<exercise xml:id="if-activity-3">
	<title>Minimum of Three Values</title>

	<p>
		Suppose <pf>list</pf> is a vector of three numbers. Set <pf>min_val</pf> to the smallest of the three values
		(without using <pf>min</pf>).
	</p>

	<program language="matlab">
if list(1) &lt;= list(2) &amp;&amp; list(1) &lt;= list(3)
		min_val = list(1);
elseif list(2) &lt;= list(3)
		min_val = list(2);
else
		min_val = list(3);
end
	</program>

	<p>
		This illustrates a useful design principle: order your conditions so that each branch excludes the ones
		that come after it. After the first test fails, you already know <pf>list(1)</pf> is not the minimum, so the
		remaining logic only needs to compare the other two entries.
	</p>
</exercise>

<!-- Exercise f-activity-6: if-elseif-else with vector indexing -->
<exercise xml:id="f-activity-6">
	<title><pf>if-elseif-else</pf></title>

	<statement>
		<p>
			<ol marker="(a)">
				<li>
					<p>
						Suppose <pf>v</pf> is a <pf>1 x 3 double</pf>. Write a code block that sets <pf>k</pf> to the index location of the first positive number inside <pf>v</pf>. If there are no positive numbers, then set <pf>k = -1</pf>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						How does the code change if <pf>k</pf> is the index of the last positive number in <pf>v</pf> instead of the first?
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>
	</statement>

	<solution component="example-solution-dev">

		<p>
			The logic is as follows: 
			<ul marker="square">
				<li>"if the 1st value positive, then set k = 1",</li>
				<li>"if the 2nd value positive, then set k = 2",</li>
				<li>"is the 3rd value positive, then set k = 3",</li>
				<li>"otherwise, set k = -1"</li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>
			In code, this looks like:

		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			<code>
				v = [-3 5 1];
				if v(1) > 0
					k = 1;
				elseif v(2) > 0
					k = 2;
				elseif v(3) > 0
					k = 3;
				else
					k = -1;
				end
				disp(k)
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			Running this code block displays <pf>k = 2</pf>.
		</p>

	</solution>

	<solution component="example-solution-dev">
		<p> 
			Recall that MATLAB only runs the commands of the FIRST TRUE branch of the if-statement and ignores the rest. Here are two reasonable approaches:

			<dl width="narrow">
				<li>
					<title>Approach 1</title>
					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							% Reverse the order
							v = [-3 5 1];

							if v(3) > 0
								k = 3;
							elseif v(2) > 0
								k = 2;
							elseif v(1) > 0
								k = 1;
							else
								k = -1;
							end
							disp(k)
						</code>
					</program>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Approach 2</title>
					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							% Split the ifs
							v = [-3 5 1];
							k = -1;		% set the default k

							if v(1) > 0
								k = 1;	% override k if true
							end

							if v(2) > 0
								k = 2;	% override k if true
							end
							
							if v(3) > 0
								k = 3;	% override k if true
							end
							disp(k)
						</code>
					</program>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>
	</solution>

</exercise>

<!-- Array-specific content from subsec-if-logical-operators -->
<p>
	When you need to test array conditions, compute the logical array first and then summarize it.
</p>

<program language="matlab">
A = [-2 0 5 7];

if any(A &lt; 0)
		disp("A contains at least one negative entry.");
end
</program>

<!-- Array-specific content from subsubsec-short-circuit-operators -->
<p>
	Use <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> and <pf>||</pf> in conditional statements (like <pf>if</pf>-statements) where you need a single true/false answer.
	Use <pf>&amp;</pf> and <pf>|</pf> when working with arrays of logical values.
</p>

<program language="matlab">
	x = 4;

	% Scalar logic (typical in conditionals)
	(x &gt; 0) &amp;&amp; (x &lt; 10)

	% Array logic (elementwise)
	A = [-2 0 5 7];
	(A &gt; 0) &amp; (A &lt; 6)     % true where A is between 1 and 5
</program>

<p>
	Short-circuit evaluation can prevent errors. For example, checking if an array is non-empty before indexing:
</p>

<program language="matlab">
	% This prevents indexing errors when A is empty
	if ~isempty(A) &amp;&amp; A(1) &lt; 0
		disp("First entry is negative.")
	end
</program>

<!-- From subsec-if-best-practices -->
<p>
	Two habits prevent a lot of bugs:
	first, make your conditions explicit and readable; second, test edge cases (like zeros, empty arrays,
	or unexpected input types).
</p>
